BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:slot-20261013-1800@devonstpoker
DTSTAMP:20261015T164520Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/London:20261013T180000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/London:20261013T181500
SUMMARY:✅ 💬 Drama: The $2.7 million in fake chips a toilet gave away (Christian Lusardi\, Borgata 2014)
DESCRIPTION:Status: POSTED\nPost: https://x.com/DevonStPoker/status/2110052950902980749\nClip slug: drama-01\nClip status: render NOT found on box\nPost text (as scheduled on X):\nA man flushed $2.7 million in fake poker chips down a hotel toilet. The toilet is what caught him.\n\nIn January 2014\, the Borgata casino in Atlantic City was running its Winter Poker Open. Two days in\, guests at the nearby Harrah's hotel reported a sewer leak in their rooms.\n\nStaff traced it to poker chips in the pipes: 494 grey $5\,000 chips and nine $25\,000 chips. All Borgata tournament chips. All fake.\n\nProsecutors said Christian Lusardi\, a player from North Carolina\, ordered the chips online from a manufacturer in China and stuck counterfeit Borgata logos on them. An audit found 160 fake $5\,000 chips\, $800\,000 in face value\, had already been slipped into the tournament. Fearing he might be caught\, prosecutors said\, he flushed the rest.\n\nThe tournament was cancelled after three days. Lusardi pleaded guilty to trademark counterfeiting and criminal mischief and in October 2015 was sentenced to five years in prison. He was ordered to pay $463\,540 to the Borgata and $9\,455 to Harrah's for the plumbing.\n\n"Lusardi was playing with dirty money long before he flushed those chips down the toilet\," said Colonel Rick Fuentes of the New Jersey State Police.\n\nHere is the part most people miss: tournament chips can't be cashed in. So who was he stealing from? Everyone else in the tournament. Every chip is a slice of one shared prize pool. Add fake chips and every honest player's slice shrinks\, even though nobody touched their wallet.\n\nIf a crime takes a little from thousands of people instead of a lot from one person\, does it feel less wrong?\nNote: replies on this post must never state allegations as fact\; attach Clip Lab key-moment clip if ready before slot
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
CATEGORIES:community
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:slot-20261013-2130@devonstpoker
DTSTAMP:20261015T164520Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/London:20261013T213000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/London:20261013T214500
SUMMARY:✅ 💬 Would You Bet? Flopped full house 7-7 on 7-2-2
DESCRIPTION:Status: POSTED\nPost: https://x.com/DevonStPoker/status/2110105796013097294\nClip slug: filler-77-boat-slowplay\nClip status: render NOT found on box\nPost text (as scheduled on X):\nYou flop a full house. 7-7 on a 7-2-2 board.\n\nDo you bet straight away\, or check and hope they catch something?\n\nThere's no wrong answer\, but there's a best one.\nNote: Filler for held rules-exposed-card (Clip Lab money-shot fail)\; no self-reply
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
CATEGORIES:community
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:slot-20261014-1800@devonstpoker
DTSTAMP:20261015T164520Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/London:20261014T180000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/London:20261014T181500
SUMMARY:✅ 💬 Drama: Black Friday: the day $390 million became a promise (Full Tilt\, April 15\, 2011)
DESCRIPTION:Status: POSTED\nPost: https://x.com/DevonStPoker/status/2110415342090514785\nClip slug: drama-02-text\nClip status: render NOT found on box\nPost text (as scheduled on X):\nOn April 15\, 2011\, American online poker players logged in and found a US government seizure notice. One site\, prosecutors later said\, owed players $390 million and had about $60 million in the bank.\n\nThat day\, federal prosecutors in Manhattan unsealed charges against people behind the three biggest poker sites serving the US: PokerStars\, Full Tilt Poker and Absolute Poker. The charges included bank fraud\, illegal gambling and money laundering. Players called it Black Friday.\n\nMost players assumed their balances were sitting safely in an account somewhere. At Full Tilt\, prosecutors alleged\, they weren't.\n\nIn September 2011\, the government alleged that Full Tilt owed players around $390 million worldwide but had roughly $60 million on hand\, and that it had paid more than $440 million to its owners\, including famous pros.\n\n"Full Tilt was not a legitimate poker company\, but a global Ponzi scheme\," said US Attorney Preet Bharara.\n\nThe outcomes differed. CEO Ray Bitar pleaded guilty in 2013 and agreed to forfeit $40 million\; the judge spared him prison because of a serious heart condition. Board members Howard Lederer and Chris Ferguson settled civil cases without admitting wrongdoing.\n\nPlayers were eventually rescued by a rival. In 2012\, PokerStars agreed a $731 million settlement with the US government\, without admitting wrongdoing\, and took over Full Tilt. By 2016\, about 45\,000 US players had been repaid roughly $114 million.\n\nThe lesson reaches well beyond poker: a balance on a screen is a promise\, not money in your hand. It is only worth as much as the honesty of whoever holds it.\n\nWhere do you keep money that you simply assume is safe?\nNote: text-only\, clip held for Ben approval
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
CATEGORIES:community
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:slot-20261014-2130@devonstpoker
DTSTAMP:20261015T164520Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/London:20261014T213000
