SUMMARY:✅ 💬 Teach line: position (ace-jack button vs UTG)
DESCRIPTION:Status: POSTED\nPost: https://x.com/DevonStPoker/status/2110468184029450735\nClip slug: filler-position-ace-jack\nClip status: render NOT found on box\nPost text (as scheduled on X):\nace-jack on the button and ace-jack under the gun are basically different hands.\n\nlast to act means you see everyone's move before you make yours. that info is free\, every single hand.\n\nwhich seat do you hate playing from most?\nNote: Filler for held rules-joke-raise-binding (Liam re-voice now ready\, rebook later)\; scheduled 2026-10-14 08:20 by daily queue\; no self-reply
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
CATEGORIES:community
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:slot-20261015-1800@devonstpoker
DTSTAMP:20261015T164520Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/London:20261015T180000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/London:20261015T181500
SUMMARY:💬 Drama: Phil Ivey's £7.7 million that Britain's Supreme Court called cheating (edge sorting\, Crockfords & Borgata)
DESCRIPTION:Status: SCHEDULED on X (seen in Poker Poster's scheduled queue)\nClip slug: drama-03-text\nClip status: render NOT found on box\nPost text (as scheduled on X):\nPhil Ivey won £7.7 million at a London casino in two days. The casino refused to pay. Five years later\, Britain's highest court said the casino was right.\n\nIvey is one of the most successful poker players ever. But in August 2012\, at Crockfords in Mayfair\, he was playing Punto Banco\, a form of baccarat where you simply bet on which hand will win. It is supposed to be pure chance.\n\nIvey and his associate\, Cheung Yin Sun\, used a technique called edge sorting. Some playing cards have tiny printing differences along their edges. Sun\, presenting it as superstition\, asked the dealer to turn certain cards around. The dealer had no idea why. Once the deck was set up\, Ivey could tell high cards from low ones before they were turned over.\n\nHe won about £7.7 million. Crockfords never paid.\n\nIvey sued. He openly described what he had done and insisted it was legitimate advantage play\, not cheating. The trial judge accepted that he genuinely believed that. In October 2017\, the UK Supreme Court unanimously ruled against him anyway.\n\n"He achieved the same result by duping the croupier\, and that is cheating as well\," Lord Hughes said\, summarising the judgment.\n\nA separate fight followed in America. Ivey and Sun had won $9.6 million at the Borgata in Atlantic City the same way. The casino paid\, then sued. A federal judge found no fraud but ruled they had broken their contract and ordered them to repay about $10.1 million. The sides settled in 2020 on undisclosed terms.\n\nThe idea worth keeping: spotting something others missed is skill. Quietly arranging the game so you can spot it is something else.\n\nWhere would you draw the line between being clever and being unfair?\nNote: text-only\, clip held for Ben approval
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
CATEGORIES:community
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
