BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:slot-20261017-1800@devonstpoker
DTSTAMP:20261015T164520Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/London:20261017T180000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/London:20261017T181500
SUMMARY:💬 Drama: Stu Ungar: $1 million\, then $800 (the greatest player who couldn't keep a dollar)
DESCRIPTION:Status: SCHEDULED on X (seen in Poker Poster's scheduled queue)\nClip slug: drama-05\nClip status: render NOT found on box\nPost text (as scheduled on X):\nStu Ungar won $1 million at the 1997 World Series of Poker. Eighteen months later\, he was found dead in a Las Vegas motel room with $800 in his pocket.\n\nUngar grew up on Manhattan's Lower East Side and was gambling for a living at 14\, beating gin rummy players several times his age.\n\nIn 1980 he entered his first Texas hold'em tournament: the World Series of Poker Main Event. He won it. He won it again in 1981.\n\nThen the years got away from him. He won and lost millions\, and struggled with drug addiction. By 1997\, according to the Las Vegas Sun\, he needed a friend to pay his way into the Main Event.\n\nHe won it a third time\, for $1 million in cash. On the broadcast\, he was asked if he would do things differently now.\n\n"There's nobody that ever beat me playing cards\," he said. "The only one that ever beat me was myself in my bad habits."\n\nTwo months later\, after paying some debts and losing heavily on horse and sports bets\, he was broke again. On November 22\, 1998\, he was found dead at the Oasis Motel\, aged 45. The coroner ruled the death accidental\, caused by heart disease brought on by his lifestyle.\n\nPoker has a word for what Ungar never managed: bankroll. It means keeping the money you play with separate from the money you live on\, and only risking what you can afford to lose. Being the best player at the table does not protect you from losing everything away from it.\n\nIs it harder to be brilliant at something\, or to be disciplined about it?\nNote: replies on this post must never state allegations as fact\; attach Clip Lab key-moment clip if ready before slot
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
CATEGORIES:community
END:VEVENT
