DTSTAMP:20261015T164520Z
DTSTART;TZID=Europe/London:20261018T180000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/London:20261018T181500
SUMMARY:💬 Drama: The $86 bluff that started the poker boom (Chris Moneymaker vs Sam Farha\, 2003)
DESCRIPTION:Status: SCHEDULED on X (seen in Poker Poster's scheduled queue)\nClip slug: drama-06\nClip status: render NOT found on box\nPost text (as scheduled on X):\nAn accountant turned an $86 online tournament into $2.5 million. Then millions of people decided they could do it too.\n\nIn 2003\, Chris Moneymaker was a 27-year-old accountant from Tennessee. He had never played a live poker tournament.\n\nHe entered an $86 qualifier on PokerStars. It won him a seat in a bigger qualifier\, which won him a $10\,000 seat at the World Series of Poker Main Event in Las Vegas. For years the story said $39. PokerStars' own records later showed $86.\n\nThere were 839 players. Moneymaker reached the final two against Sam Farha\, a seasoned pro.\n\nThen came the hand. Moneymaker had nothing: king-high\, no pair. Farha had a pair of nines\, the better hand. On the last card\, Moneymaker bet everything. That is a bluff: betting big with a weak hand to make your opponent believe you are strong.\n\nFarha thought it over and folded the winning hand.\n\n"I know we're early in the century\, but that's the bluff of the century\," said ESPN commentator Norman Chad.\n\nMoneymaker went on to win the title and $2.5 million. Three years later\, the Main Event drew 8\,773 players. The poker boom had a face.\n\nThe lesson of that hand is not "be fearless". A bluff only works if the story makes sense. Moneymaker's earlier bets fitted a strong hand\, and the cards on the table offered several. Farha wasn't beaten by courage. He was beaten by a believable story.\n\nHave you ever been talked out of something you were right about\, just because the other person seemed so sure?\nNote: replies on this post must never state allegations as fact\; attach Clip Lab key-moment clip if ready before slot\; clip not attached natively (third-party ESPN upload\, copyright risk)
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
CATEGORIES:community
END:VEVENT
