SUMMARY:💬 Drama: Jamie Gold's $12 million and the voicemail that froze half of it (2006 WSOP)
DESCRIPTION:Status: SCHEDULED on X (seen in Poker Poster's scheduled queue)\nClip slug: drama-08\nClip status: render NOT found on box\nPost text (as scheduled on X):\nJamie Gold won $12 million at the World Series of Poker. Before he could collect it all\, a court froze half.\n\nIn 2006\, the World Series of Poker Main Event was the biggest it had ever been: 8\,773 players. Gold\, a former Hollywood talent agent\, outlasted them all and won $12 million.\n\nWithin weeks\, TV producer Crispin Leyser sued him. Leyser said Gold had promised him half the winnings in return for recruiting celebrities to wear a poker site's logo at the tournament.\n\nHis evidence was a voicemail. On the final day of the tournament\, according to Card Player\, Gold left a message promising Leyser "your half".\n\nGold had already withdrawn $6 million. A court froze the other $6 million\, which sat in the Rio casino's vault while lawyers argued. In December 2006\, the judge indicated Leyser would likely win his claim.\n\nIn February 2007 they settled. The terms were never disclosed. Their joint statement said: "Jamie always intended on sharing his winnings with Crispin."\n\nGold later called it a misunderstanding that should never have reached court.\n\nThis is one of poker's oldest problems. Players constantly make side deals: splitting winnings\, swapping percentages\, backing each other. Most are handshakes or text messages. They feel friendly when you make them. They feel very different once one side is suddenly holding $12 million.\n\nThe fix is boring and it works: if a promise could ever be worth real money\, write down exactly what it means before anyone wins.\n\nHave you ever made a casual promise that would look completely different if it were suddenly worth millions?\nNote: replies on this post must never state allegations as fact\; attach Clip Lab key-moment clip if ready before slot
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
CATEGORIES:community
END:VEVENT
