DTSTART;TZID=Europe/London:20261021T180000
DTEND;TZID=Europe/London:20261021T181500
SUMMARY:💬 Drama: Polk vs Negreanu: the grudge match that took 25\,000 hands to settle
DESCRIPTION:Status: SCHEDULED on X (seen in Poker Poster's scheduled queue)\nClip slug: drama-10\nClip status: render NOT found on box\nPost text (as scheduled on X):\nTwo poker stars who openly disliked each other agreed to settle it at the table. 25\,000 hands later\, one of them was $1.2 million richer.\n\nDoug Polk and Daniel Negreanu had traded insults online for years. Polk had made his name in heads-up poker\, meaning one-on-one. Negreanu is one of the most famous players in the world\, but heads-up was not his speciality.\n\nSo they set terms: 25\,000 hands of one-on-one online poker at $200/$400 blinds\, stakes where pots regularly reached tens of thousands of dollars.\n\nBetting markets made Polk roughly a 4-to-1 favourite. Negreanu had big days\, including one session where he won nearly $400\,000. But over 36 sessions and three months\, Polk pulled ahead and stayed there. In February 2021 he finished up $1\,201\,807.\n\n"He's a great player and obviously the favorite going in\," Negreanu said afterwards. Polk returned the compliment: "If you're not a heads-up pro\, he's going to beat you."\n\nBy the end\, the two had shared a study session and\, in Polk's words\, buried the hatchet.\n\nThe real lesson is in the number of hands. In poker\, a weaker player can win a session\, a day\, even a week. Luck is loud over short stretches. Skill only shows up clearly over thousands of hands. That is why they agreed to 25\,000 hands\, not 250.\n\nThe match settled a feud. It also showed how long you have to watch before you can tell luck from skill.\n\nWhat have you judged too quickly\, before you had seen enough results to really know?\nNote: replies on this post must never state allegations as fact\; attach Clip Lab key-moment clip if ready before slot
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
CATEGORIES:community
END:VEVENT
