SUMMARY:💬 Drama: Annette Obrestad WSOPE 2007
DESCRIPTION:Status: SCHEDULED on X (seen in Poker Poster's scheduled queue)\nClip slug: drama-11-obrestad\nClip status: render NOT found on box\nPost text (as scheduled on X):\nAn 18-year-old won £1 million at the World Series of Poker. In Las Vegas\, she would have been too young to enter.\n\nIn September 2007\, the World Series of Poker held its first European edition\, in London. The age rules were different there. In Las Vegas\, you had to be 21 to play. In London\, younger players could enter.\n\nAnnette Obrestad\, a Norwegian teenager\, signed up for the £10\,000 Main Event. Live poker for money was banned in Norway at the time. She had learned online\, where she was known as Annette_15. Before London\, she had cashed in only four live tournaments.\n\nOver five days\, she outlasted 361 other players\, including some of the best professionals in Europe.\n\nIn the final hand\, she held a pair of sevens against John Tabatabai\, a 22-year-old from London. The first three shared cards gave her three sevens and gave him two pair. She moved all in\, he called\, and he was beaten before the last card was even dealt.\n\n"I never expected to win\," she said. "I'm speechless. I really don't know what to say."\n\nShe won £1 million\, about $2 million at the time\, the biggest single payday a woman had ever had in poker. The day before her 19th birthday\, she became the youngest World Series bracelet winner in history.\n\nIt was almost three more years before she was old enough to play the World Series in Las Vegas.\n\nThe idea: experience is measured in decisions\, not years. Online\, a player can see more hands in a few months than many live players see in years. Obrestad was young. She was not inexperienced.\n\nWhat skill have you seen someone master far younger than you expected?
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
CATEGORIES:community
END:VEVENT
